+```json
+{"schema_version":1,"plan_id":"ui","node_id":"U0b","attempt":2,"source":"gate","source_ref":"gate.red event at head 8d2bb50 in docs/trace/ui/U0b/loop.jsonl","findings":[{"id":"I1","rule_id":"DATA-1","layer":"tests","files":["src/web/tsconfig.json","src/web/playwright.config.ts","tests/web/e2e/U0b/"],"root_cause":"This is one cause, and it blocks all 11 rule greps (DATA-1, DATA-3, DATA-5, DATA-6, DATA-7, THEME-1, SEC-1, BRAND-1, HONEST-1, A11Y-1, RESP-1). I have deliberately kept it as one finding, filed under DATA-1, the first node rule. Symptom: playwright.config.ts:1 does a named import of `defineConfig` from '@playwright/test', and Node throws a SyntaxError before any test is collected, so each grep reports 0 failed and 0 passed. Cause: src/web/tsconfig.json:20-25 sets a catch-all `paths` mapping, \"*\" to [\"./node_modules/@types/*\", \"./node_modules/*\"]. Playwright's loader applies tsconfig `paths` to bare specifiers in .ts files (node_modules/playwright/lib/transform/esmLoader.js:7792-7826). So '@playwright/test' is rewritten to the directory src/web/node_modules/@playwright/test. That directory is then resolved with afterPathMapping=true (esmLoader.js:7619-7622), which looks only at `main` (absent) and then `index` with '.js' tried first (esmLoader.js:7597). The result is the CommonJS index.js, not the ESM index.mjs that the package's `exports.import` condition names (node_modules/@playwright/test/package.json:20). index.js:17 is `module.exports = require('playwright/test')`, and playwright/test.js:24 builds its exports with `Object.assign(...)`. Node's CJS named-export detection cannot see names built that way, so `defineConfig` is not a named export when an ES module (package.json:4 is \"type\": \"module\") imports it. Every spec and support file under tests/web/e2e/U0b/ named-imports `test`/`expect` from '@playwright/test' (for example data-1.spec.ts:4 and support/app.ts:7), so they would fail the same way. Changing only the config's import would fix the symptom, not the cause. The mapping exists so that tsc can resolve bare imports from tests/web/** (outside the package) to src/web/node_modules, and it was in the scaffold. gate.scaffold (0f2d3c6) ran typecheck and build but never loaded Playwright, so nothing caught it. Fix direction: the typecheck still needs to resolve tests/web/** imports to the package's node_modules, but Playwright's runtime must stop resolving package specifiers through this wildcard. Either Playwright loads under a tsconfig without the catch-all package mapping, or the mapping is narrowed so it no longer turns installed packages into directory paths, letting the package `exports` (import → .mjs) resolve. This is a scaffold-level config change in src/web/, not a test change. No test may be weakened.","fingerprint":"DATA-1|src/web/tsconfig.json|paths-wildcard-routes-playwright-to-cjs-index","testable":true,"suggested_test":"Given the committed tree at the red head with npm ci done in src/web, When `npm --prefix src/web run e2e -- ../../tests/web/e2e/U0b --list` runs, Then the config loads with no SyntaxError and at least one test is listed for each of the 11 '<ID> · ' tags; and When each '--grep \"<ID> · \"' run executes, Then it reports at least one failed test whose failure is an assertion, not a module or resolution error (gate.red pre-check: playwright --list over tests/web/e2e/U0b)","untestable_reason":null},{"id":"I2","rule_id":"DATA-1","layer":"tests","files":["src/web/package.json","src/web/tsconfig.json"],"root_cause":"This cause is separate from I1. It did not cause this gate failure, but it makes the typecheck gate depend on the machine it runs on. Symptom (the web-tester's report, confirmed): `npm --prefix src/web run typecheck` passes only because tsc finds @types/node in a node_modules directory above the checkout, outside the repo. Cause: src/web/package.json:24-40 (devDependencies) has no @types/node, and src/web/node_modules/@types/ contains only esrecurse, deep-eql, json-schema, chai, estree, react, react-dom and aria-query. Yet the code being typechecked needs Node types: tsconfig.json:17-19 loads `vite/client`, and tsconfig.json:27-37 includes tests/web/e2e, whose support files import node: built-ins (for example tests/web/e2e/U0b/support/trail.ts:5-9 and support/app.ts:4-5). tsc's type-reference lookup walks up through ancestor node_modules folders, and tsconfig.json:22 sends `@types/*` only to the package's own node_modules, where @types/node is missing. So the lookup falls through to whatever an ancestor directory holds. gate.scaffold's typecheck exit 0 (loop.jsonl line 3) came from a scratch directory where such an ancestor was reachable. On a clean machine or in CI, the same tree would fail to compile. That would be a compile error, which is not red. Fix direction: the Node type definitions must come from src/web's own lockfile-pinned devDependencies, not from the environment. @types/node is not on react-web's approved dev-package list, so David must rule on adding it (and pin a version) before the builder touches package.json.","fingerprint":"DATA-1|src/web/package.json|types-node-resolved-outside-repo","testable":true,"suggested_test":"Given a git archive of the red head extracted to a directory with no node_modules in any ancestor directory, and npm ci run in its src/web, When `npm --prefix src/web run typecheck` runs, Then it exits 0 and every Node type it uses resolves to a file under that src/web/node_modules (gate.scaffold / gate.red typecheck in an isolated scratch)","untestable_reason":null}],"previous_attempts":["attempt 1: t1 scaffold gate.scaffold PASS at 0f2d3c6: typecheck and build only, so Playwright never loaded and the tsconfig paths wildcard (I1) went unexercised","attempt 1: t2 red gate.red FAIL at 8d2bb50: all 11 rule greps exit 1 with 0 failed and 0 passed, because playwright.config.ts cannot load (SyntaxError: no named export defineConfig). The cause is the I1 tsconfig paths wildcard, first seen at this attempt, so it has no prior fingerprint","attempt 1: the web-tester reported that typecheck resolves @types/node from outside the repo; this is confirmed as I2, a separate cause that needs David's ruling on the package"]}
