e, so they are filed once, said openly. Symptom: commit trailer values (Agent, Plan, Node, Rule, TDD and Fixes-Finding) and compiledAt.ref reach trace.json without passing the denylist. Cause: the scan runs on the compiler's inputs, not on what it writes. The read() helper scans every docs file it reads (index.ts:88-93), and one extra scan covers the commit subject (index.ts:111). Values from git that come any other way are never scanned: the trailer values emitted at index.ts:112-124 and the ref emitted at :206. The compile fails only on hits from those input scans (:202). DATA-5 (docs/ui-requirements.md:112) says 'The compiler fails the build if its output matches the privacy denylist'. That is about the output, and the output is not fully covered. The commit-msg hook does not close the gap. It checks Agent against a fixed list (commit-msg:22), Plan and Node against the branch (:24-25), Fixes-Finding against a pattern (:28), and TDD only on tester and builder commits (:31-42). Rule is checked only for being non-empty (:35), so it can hold free text. The hook also never runs on commits made elsewhere: David's GitHub UI merges, or any ref the compiler is pointed at. The ref comes from the command line (index.ts:217-218), and a branch name may hold '@'. Exposure is narrow, so it is not a leak today, but the rule's guarantee is not met. First occurrence, no earlier fingerprint. Fix direction: every value the compiler writes must be covered by the denylist. Either scan every value taken from git that it emits (each trailer value and the ref) with a location naming the commit, or scan the serialised trace.json and chunks once more before writing. Either way, keep the file:line naming for hits in trail files and never print the matched text. Blocking for U0b: yes. DATA-5 is a MUST rule of this node and the repo goes public. Order: I4's red test lands first.","fingerprint":"DATA-5|src/web/compiler/index.ts|scan-wired-to-inputs-trailers-and-ref-emitted-unscanned","testable